  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule. The rule is enabled and its first run succeeded.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each record is one web request, with these main fields:\n- **Request:** `request`, `url`, `extension`, `referer`\n- **Response:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as text with a `.keyword` version) and `bytes`\n- **Client:** `clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`, `machine.ram`\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n- **Other:** `host`, `tags`, `memory`, and a computed `hour_of_day` field\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-0e60c973 Web logs overview\"** (id `08758e64-1e0b-465f-b140-f3ae12c50851`, default time range last 7 days) has 7 panels:\n- Four single-number tiles: total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`), average response size in bytes, and number of 5xx error requests\n- Requests over time, stacked by response code\n- Top 10 requested URLs\n- Top 10 source countries\n\nI read the dashboard back from the API and all panels were saved as sent. I haven't opened it in the browser to check that the charts draw correctly.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-0e60c973 5xx spike\"** (id `5c362f0d-2de4-4b36-be68-b6b8caa8341d`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- It checks every minute.\n- It counts requests whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" over the last 5 minutes.\n- It fires when that count is more than 10.\n\nThe rule has no actions, so it records alerts in Kibana but won't send any notifications. You can add an email or Slack connector to it if you want them.",
